# tinyqueue

A small, dependency free priority queue for Node.js and the browser. It is a binary min-heap with a comparator you can swap out, and it is built for the case where you push and pop a lot of items and care about allocation.

## Install

```sh
npm install tinyqueue
```

## Quick start

```js
import TinyQueue from 'tinyqueue';

const queue = new TinyQueue([], (a, b) => a.priority - b.priority);

queue.push({ name: 'write docs', priority: 3 });
queue.push({ name: 'fix flaky test', priority: 1 });
queue.push({ name: 'bump version', priority: 2 });

queue.peek();   // { name: 'fix flaky test', priority: 1 }
queue.pop();    // removes and returns the same object
queue.length;   // 2
```

## API

### `new TinyQueue(items = [], compare = defaultCompare)`

Creates a queue. If `items` is given, the queue is built in O(n) with a bottom-up heapify, which is faster than pushing the items one at a time. The array is copied, so the original is not modified.

`compare(a, b)` must return a negative number if `a` should come out before `b`, a positive number if after, and zero if they are equal. The default compares with `<` and `>`, which is fine for numbers and strings.

### `queue.push(item)`

Adds an item in O(log n).

### `queue.pop()`

Removes and returns the smallest item, or `undefined` when the queue is empty. O(log n).

### `queue.peek()`

Returns the smallest item without removing it, or `undefined` when empty. O(1).

### `queue.length`

Number of items currently stored. This is a plain property, not a getter, so reading it is free.

## Examples

### Merge k sorted lists

```js
function mergeSorted(lists) {
  const q = new TinyQueue([], (a, b) => a.value - b.value);
  lists.forEach((list, i) => {
    if (list.length) q.push({ value: list[0], list: i, index: 0 });
  });

  const out = [];
  while (q.length) {
    const { value, list, index } = q.pop();
    out.push(value);
    const next = index + 1;
    if (next < lists[list].length) {
      q.push({ value: lists[list][next], list, index: next });
    }
  }
  return out;
}

mergeSorted([[1, 4, 9], [2, 3, 10], [5]]);
// [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 9, 10]
```

### Dijkstra on a small graph

```js
function shortestPaths(graph, source) {
  const dist = new Map([[source, 0]]);
  const q = new TinyQueue([[source, 0]], (a, b) => a[1] - b[1]);

  while (q.length) {
    const [node, d] = q.pop();
    if (d > (dist.get(node) ?? Infinity)) continue; // stale entry
    for (const [next, weight] of graph[node] ?? []) {
      const nd = d + weight;
      if (nd < (dist.get(next) ?? Infinity)) {
        dist.set(next, nd);
        q.push([next, nd]);
      }
    }
  }
  return dist;
}
```

The queue has no decrease-key operation. The usual trick, shown above, is to push a new entry and skip stale ones when they are popped.

## Performance notes

On a 2021 laptop, pushing and popping one million random integers takes about 280 ms. Memory use is one array slot per item plus the items themselves. There are no per-item wrapper objects.

## Stability

The heap is not stable: items that compare equal may come out in any order. If you need first in, first out behaviour for ties, add a sequence number to your items and use it as a secondary key in the comparator.

## Browser support

The package ships ES modules and a UMD build. It uses no syntax newer than ES2015 in the UMD file.

## Contributing

Run `npm test` before opening a pull request. New behaviour needs a test, and the benchmark in `bench/` should not regress by more than five percent.

